  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe active weather overnight shifted south into Iowa and Illinois \nwith the main front. We've seen clearing slowly proceed south \novernight, but we are expecting any clear skies this morning to \ncloud back up pretty quick as a stratocu field fills back in with \nmixing. Still, the presence of at least a little sun will allow \nhighs today to push up into the mid 60s. \n\nFor tonight and Monday, energy from the messy trough on water vapor \nimagery moving across MT/WY will slowly move across the region. The \nstrongest bit of energy, which is currently over WY, will continue \neast across Neb and into Iowa on Monday. This will spread more \nclouds across the area, with light rain looking likely to spread \nacross southern MN on Monday given broad and weak ascent associated \nwith the h5 low down over Iowa. Amounts will be very light (under a \ntenth of an inch), but Monday will be another cool, gray, and damp \nday south of the Twin Cities. \n\nFor the rest of the work week, we'll see a Rex Block try to develop \nover central Canada, with the primary blocking high setting up over \nHudson Bay. This upper air setup will support a nearly station \nsurface high over the Great Lakes, with a dry easterly flow \nresulting for us. This will dry us out for Tuesday and Wednesday. \n\nWednesday night, a cold front will be moving across the Dakotas. \nThis front will washout and lose its definition over MN on Thursday, \nbut its plume of increased moisture will remain, which looks to \nshake out some light rain. The best chances will be in western MN as \nthe low level air becomes increasingly drier the farther east you go.\n\nFor Friday into the weekend, we'll see ridging return with upper \nflow becoming southwesterly in response to height falls across \nwestern North America. The forecast for next weekend is trending \ndrier as ensemble means show that western trough not ejecting out \nacross the central CONUS until early in the week of the 28th. After \nthe gloomy weekend we have had to this point, next weekend should be \na great weekend to get out and enjoy your favorite fall outdoor \nactivities with highs getting back up into the low to mid 70s.",
